wwv_flow_imp_page.create_page_plug(
 p_id=>wwv_flow_imp.id(235758358095453278457)
,p_plug_name=>'Instructions (click arrow to show / hide)'
,p_region_template_options=>'#DEFAULT#:is-collapsed:t-Region--scrollBody'
,p_plug_template=>2664334895415463485
,p_plug_display_sequence=>10
,p_location=>null
,p_plug_source=>wwv_flow_string.join(wwv_flow_t_varchar2(
'<span style="font-size:14px; font-weight:bold;">',
'<p>',
'    Why would I want to use Program Options?',
'    </p>',
'</span>',
'<p>Program options provide a way for developers to regulate application behavior without making changes to the code.  Think of them as Global Substitution variables that you can control without changing your application.  The values are stored in a d'
||'atabase, and can be altered by executing this APEX Application.',
'</p>',
'<span style="font-size:14px; font-weight:bold;">',
'    <p>',
'    Here are some examples of how Program Options have been employed:',
'    </p>',
'</span>',
'<ul>',
'    <li>Maintain a "Test Mode" switch (BOOLEAN) outside the application that controls whether e-mails are sent by the app.  Great for testing.</li>',
'    <li>Same app as above has an additional program option that contains a ID to send ALL e-mails to if "Test Mode" is TRUE.</li>',
'    <li>Maybe you have need to build several apps that send multiple kinds of e-mails.  The body text of each of these e-mails changes regularly.  Sure, you could build a table for each app to house the HTML body text, but why when you can use someth'
||'ing that''s already built that you can reuse for ANY application?</li>',
'    <li>You have a pop-up List of Values with certain values the user wants to exclude and they change from time to time.  Use a NUMBER program option to store the key(s) of the rows you want to exclude and put the check in your SQL WHERE clause.</li'
||'>',
'</ul>',
'</br>',
'<p>Options are stored by either App ID (#) or App Alias to provide flexibility, depending on how you version your applications.  You can have multiple options for each application, as "Option Number" is part of the key to each row.',
'<p>',
'    Options are retrieved by your application by calling an "overloaded" PL/SQL function that is granted to all the APEX workspaces.',
'</p>',
'</br>',
'<span style="font-size:14px; font-weight:bold;">',
'<p>Generic option types made available in this app are:</p>',
'</span>',
'<ol>',
'<li>Text / VARCHAR (Basic text, letters and numbers.  Great for substituting)',
'<li>NUMBER (Literally a ''NUMBER'' data type in Oracle)',
'<li>DATE',
'<li>TIMESTAMP',
'<li>BOOLEAN (Stored as text on database, but returned as a BOOLEAN to the App)',
'<li>CLOB (A really big VARCHAR container.  Great for storing HTML formatted text for dropping into e-mails or on-screen instructions.  Created / maintained using a Rich Text editor.)</li>',
'</ol>',
'<p>',
'    Each of the above types are available on EACH row.  That means if you need one VARCHAR option and one DATE option, you can store them as two separate rows (Option #1 = VARCHAR and Option #2 = DATE) OR you can store ONE option row and populate bot'
||'h the VARCHAR and DATE columns.  This all gets sorted out in how you call the function. ',
'</p>',
'<p>',
'   <strong>There are more detailed instructions on subsequent screens.  <span style="color:red;">The most detailed instructions are on the screen where the program options are CREATED and MODIFIED.</span>  So, to get the full picture, go ahead and CR'
||'EATE a Program Option for your program.  You can always delete it if you don''t need it!</strong>',
'</p>',
'<p>',
'     Click the "Program Options by Application" link below to get started.  ',
'</p>'))
,p_plug_query_num_rows=>15
,p_plug_display_condition_type=>'EXPRESSION'
,p_plug_display_when_condition=>'NOT APXSHR_PGM_OPTS.pgm_opt_exists(:APP_ALIAS,to_number(:APP_PAGE_ID) + 100)'
,p_plug_display_when_cond2=>'PLSQL'
,p_attributes=>wwv_flow_t_plugin_attributes(wwv_flow_t_varchar2(
  'expand_shortcuts', 'N',
  'output_as', 'HTML',
  'show_line_breaks', 'Y')).to_clob
);
